# Sizes: scale_size

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_size()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_size.html), which covers
`scale_radius()`, `scale_size_binned()`, `scale_size_area()` and `scale_size_binned_area()` too,
in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An
example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the
page is also a list of what remains.

The `size` aesthetic is mostly for points and text. `scale_size()` maps a continuous column to their
area, since area is what readers perceive, while `scale_radius()` maps it to their radius.
`scale_size_area()` makes a value of 0 an area of 0. `scale_size_binned()` and
`scale_size_binned_area()` are the binned counterparts of `scale_size()` and `scale_size_area()`,
cutting the column into bins first. A line's width, once also set by `size`, is now the `linewidth`
aesthetic. Giggly has `Giggly.Plot.scale_size_continuous/2`, `Giggly.Plot.scale_size_area/2`,
`Giggly.Plot.scale_radius/2` and `Giggly.Plot.scale_size_discrete/2`, but not ggplot2's short name,
`scale_size()`, nor binned size scales (`scale_size_binned()`, `scale_size_binned_area()`), and by
default maps a point's `size` to a column as ggplot2's default scale does, and draws the legend that
reads it back, but not a text's `size`. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with
`Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells
write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with
`Kino.Image`. The data is `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`, ggplot2's `mpg`: fuel economy for 234 cars of 38
popular models from 1999 to 2008, which the setup cell binds to `mpg`, ggplot2's name for it.

## Size from a column

```r
p <- ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy, size = hwy)) +
   geom_point()
p
p + scale_size("Highway mpg")
p + scale_size(range = c(0, 10))
```

A scale's first argument titles its legend, and `range` sets the smallest and the largest sizes.
`scale_size()` is `Giggly.Plot.scale_size_continuous/2`, its title `name:`.

```elixir
p =
  mpg
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy, size: :hwy})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

render.(p)
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.scale_size_continuous(name: "Highway mpg")
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.scale_size_continuous(range: {0, 10})
|> render.()
```

## Zero as no size

```r
# If you want zero value to have zero size, use scale_size_area:
p + scale_size_area()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.scale_size_area()
|> render.()
```

## Binned sizes

```r
# Binning can sometimes make it easier to match the scaled data to the legend
p + scale_size_binned()
```

Not in Giggly yet: `scale_size_binned()` and the legend that reads it back.

## Sizes from counts

```r
# This is most useful when size is a count
ggplot(mpg, aes(class, cyl)) +
  geom_count() +
  scale_size_area()
```

`geom_count()` draws one point for each place that has rows, sized by how many it has.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :class, y: :cyl})
|> Plot.geom_count()
|> Plot.scale_size_area()
|> render.()
```

## Size as radius

```r
# If you want to map size to radius (usually bad idea), use scale_radius
p + scale_radius()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.scale_radius()
|> render.()
```
